model_answer_en:
(a) Inheritance is the OOP mechanism by which a new class (subclass/child) acquires the fields and methods of an existing class (superclass/parent) and can add or override members. It models an "is-a" relationship (a Car is a Vehicle), gives code reuse and enables runtime polymorphism. In Java: class B extends A.

| Type | Shape | Java support |
|---|---|---|
| Single | A → B | Yes (class B extends A) |
| Multilevel | A → B → C | Yes |
| Hierarchical | A → B, A → C | Yes |
| Multiple | A, B → C | Not with classes; yes with interfaces |
| Hybrid | Mix of the above | Only through interfaces |

(b) Diamond problem: suppose classes B and C both extend A and override show(), and class D extends both B and C. A call d.show() is ambiguous: should it run B's or C's version, and does D hold one or two copies of A's fields? To keep the language simple and avoid this ambiguity in state and behaviour, Java allows a class to extend only one class. (C++ allows it and solves it with virtual base classes.)

(c) Multiple inheritance through interfaces: a class can implement any number of interfaces. Interfaces have no instance fields, so there is no duplicated state. If two interfaces supply the same default method, the compiler forces the class to override it, and the class may pick one with InterfaceName.super.method().

interface Camera {
    void takePhoto();
    default void powerOn() { System.out.println("Camera powered on"); }
}

interface MusicPlayer {
    void playMusic();
    default void powerOn() { System.out.println("Player powered on"); }
}

class Phone {
    void call() { System.out.println("Calling..."); }
}

class SmartPhone extends Phone implements Camera, MusicPlayer {
    public void takePhoto() { System.out.println("Photo taken"); }
    public void playMusic() { System.out.println("Playing music"); }
    // both interfaces give powerOn(): the class must resolve the conflict
    public void powerOn() {
        Camera.super.powerOn();
        System.out.println("SmartPhone ready");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SmartPhone s = new SmartPhone();
        s.powerOn();
        s.call();
        s.takePhoto();
        s.playMusic();
        Camera c = s;          // a SmartPhone is-a Camera
        c.takePhoto();
    }
}

Output:
Camera powered on
SmartPhone ready
Calling...
Photo taken
Playing music
Photo taken

SmartPhone inherits call() from Phone (class inheritance) and the types Camera and MusicPlayer (interface inheritance). Without the overriding powerOn() in SmartPhone the code would not compile ("inherits unrelated defaults for powerOn()").

model_answer_bn:
(ক) Inheritance হলো OOP-এর সেই পদ্ধতি যার মাধ্যমে একটি নতুন class (subclass/child) একটি বিদ্যমান class-এর (superclass/parent) field ও method পায় এবং নতুন member যোগ বা override করতে পারে। এটি "is-a" সম্পর্ক বোঝায় (Car একটি Vehicle), code reuse দেয় এবং runtime polymorphism সম্ভব করে। Java-তে: class B extends A।

| প্রকার | গঠন | Java-তে সমর্থন |
|---|---|---|
| Single | A → B | হ্যাঁ (class B extends A) |
| Multilevel | A → B → C | হ্যাঁ |
| Hierarchical | A → B, A → C | হ্যাঁ |
| Multiple | A, B → C | Class দিয়ে নয়; interface দিয়ে হ্যাঁ |
| Hybrid | উপরেরগুলোর মিশ্রণ | শুধু interface-এর মাধ্যমে |

(খ) Diamond problem: ধরো B ও C দুটি class-ই A-কে extend করে show() override করে, এবং class D, B ও C দুটিকেই extend করে। তখন d.show() call দ্ব্যর্থক: B-এর version চলবে নাকি C-এর, এবং D-তে A-এর field-এর একটি copy থাকবে নাকি দুটি? ভাষা সহজ রাখতে এবং state ও behaviour-এর এই দ্ব্যর্থতা এড়াতে Java একটি class-কে কেবল একটি class extend করতে দেয়। (C++ এটি অনুমোদন করে এবং virtual base class দিয়ে সমাধান করে।)

(গ) Interface-এর মাধ্যমে multiple inheritance: একটি class যত খুশি interface implement করতে পারে। Interface-এ instance field নেই, তাই state দ্বিগুণ হয় না। দুটি interface একই default method দিলে compiler class-কে তা override করতে বাধ্য করে, এবং class InterfaceName.super.method() দিয়ে একটি বেছে নিতে পারে।

