model_answer_en:
Definition: A friend function is a non-member function that a class allows, through the keyword friend, to access its private and protected members. The class declares it inside its body (friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);), but the function is defined and called like an ordinary function.

Characteristics

| # | Characteristic |
|---|---|
| 1 | It is not a member of the class, so it is not called with an object and the dot operator: canClear(acc, loan), not acc.canClear() |
| 2 | It has no this pointer; it reaches members through an object passed as an argument (a.balance) |
| 3 | The friend declaration can be placed in the private, protected or public section with the same effect |
| 4 | It is defined outside the class without the scope operator (no Account::) and without the keyword friend |
| 5 | One function can be a friend of several classes (useful when it must read data of two classes) |
| 6 | Friendship is granted, not taken: it is not inherited by derived classes and is not transitive |
| 7 | A whole class can also be made a friend (friend class Auditor;) |

Example (one function reads private data of two classes):

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Account;                      // forward declaration

class Loan {
    double due;
public:
    Loan(double d) : due(d) {}
    friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);
};

class Account {
    double balance;
public:
    Account(double b) : balance(b) {}
    friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);
};

// one friend function reads private data of two classes
bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l) {
    return a.balance >= l.due;
}

int main() {
    Account acc(50000);
    Loan loan(35000);
    cout << (canClear(acc, loan) ? "Loan can be cleared" : "Not enough balance") << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Loan can be cleared

Advantages
- Lets one function work on the private data of two or more classes (operations that do not belong to either class alone).
- Needed for some operator overloads, such as operator<< for output, where the left operand (cout) is not an object of the class.
- Avoids writing many public getters just for one helper, so the data stays hidden from everyone else.

Disadvantages
- Weakens encapsulation and data hiding: code outside the class can change private data.
- The class must name every friend in advance, which couples the classes tightly; changing the private layout can break the friend.
- Friendship is not inherited, so derived classes need their own friend declarations, and overuse makes the design harder to maintain.

model_answer_bn:
সংজ্ঞা: Friend function হলো একটি non-member function, যাকে একটি class friend keyword দিয়ে নিজের private ও protected member ব্যবহারের অনুমতি দেয়। Class নিজের body-তে একে ঘোষণা করে (friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);), কিন্তু function-টি সাধারণ function-এর মতো define ও call করা হয়।

বৈশিষ্ট্য

| # | বৈশিষ্ট্য |
|---|---|
| 1 | এটি class-এর member নয়, তাই object ও dot operator দিয়ে call হয় না: canClear(acc, loan), acc.canClear() নয় |
| 2 | এর কোনো this pointer নেই; argument হিসেবে পাঠানো object দিয়ে member-এ পৌঁছায় (a.balance) |
| 3 | Friend ঘোষণা private, protected বা public যেকোনো section-এ রাখা যায়, প্রভাব একই |
| 4 | Class-এর বাইরে scope operator (Account:: নেই) ও friend keyword ছাড়া define করা হয় |
| 5 | একটি function একাধিক class-এর friend হতে পারে (দুই class-এর data পড়তে হলে কাজে লাগে) |
| 6 | Friendship দেওয়া হয়, নেওয়া যায় না: এটি derived class-এ inherit হয় না এবং transitive নয় |
| 7 | পুরো একটি class-কেও friend করা যায় (friend class Auditor;) |

উদাহরণ (একটি function দুই class-এর private data পড়ে):

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Account;                      // forward declaration

class Loan {
    double due;
public:
    Loan(double d) : due(d) {}
    friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);
};

class Account {
    double balance;
public:
    Account(double b) : balance(b) {}
    friend bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l);
};

// one friend function reads private data of two classes
bool canClear(const Account &a, const Loan &l) {
    return a.balance >= l.due;
}

int main() {
    Account acc(50000);
    Loan loan(35000);
    cout << (canClear(acc, loan) ? "Loan can be cleared" : "Not enough balance") << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Loan can be cleared

সুবিধা
- একটি function দুই বা ততোধিক class-এর private data নিয়ে কাজ করতে পারে (যে কাজ কোনো একটি class-এর একার নয়)।
- কিছু operator overload-এর জন্য দরকার, যেমন output-এর operator<<, যেখানে বাঁ দিকের operand (cout) class-এর object নয়।
- শুধু একটি helper-এর জন্য অনেক public getter লিখতে হয় না, ফলে data অন্য সবার কাছে লুকানো থাকে।

অসুবিধা
- Encapsulation ও data hiding দুর্বল করে: class-এর বাইরের code private data বদলাতে পারে।
- Class-কে আগে থেকেই প্রতিটি friend-এর নাম দিতে হয়, ফলে class-গুলো শক্তভাবে যুক্ত হয়; private গঠন বদলালে friend ভেঙে যেতে পারে।
- Friendship inherit হয় না, তাই derived class-এর জন্য আলাদা friend ঘোষণা লাগে, আর অতিরিক্ত ব্যবহারে design রক্ষণাবেক্ষণ কঠিন হয়।

model_answer_en:
Definition: Inheritance is the OOP mechanism by which a new class (the derived class, child or subclass) acquires the data members and member functions of an existing class (the base class, parent or superclass) and adds or overrides its own. It models an "is-a" relationship: an Officer is an Employee.

Syntax: class Derived : access-mode Base { ... };   (C++)   class Derived extends Base { ... }   (Java)

| Term | Meaning in the example |
|---|---|
| Base class | Employee (name, basic, show()) |
| Derived class | Officer (adds houseRent and gross()) |
| Inherited members | name, basic and show() are used by Officer without being rewritten |
| Base constructor call | Officer(...) : Employee(n, b) builds the Employee part first |

Example (C++):

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Employee {                       // base class
protected:
    string name;
    double basic;
public:
    Employee(string n, double b) : name(n), basic(b) {}
    void show() const { cout << name << ", basic " << basic << endl; }
};

class Officer : public Employee {      // derived class
    double houseRent;
public:
    Officer(string n, double b) : Employee(n, b), houseRent(b * 0.5) {}
    double gross() const { return basic + houseRent; }   // uses inherited basic
};

int main() {
    Officer o("Nusrat", 40000);
    o.show();                          // inherited from Employee
    cout << "Gross: " << o.gross() << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Nusrat, basic 40000
Gross: 60000

Officer never declares name, basic or show(); it reuses them from Employee and adds only what is new (house rent at 50% of basic, so gross = 40000 + 20000 = 60000). basic is protected, so the derived class can read it while outside code cannot.

Types of inheritance

| Type | Shape |
|---|---|
| Single | A → B |
| Multilevel | A → B → C |
| Hierarchical | A → B, A → C |
| Multiple | A, B → C (C++ only; Java uses interfaces) |
| Hybrid | a mix of two or more of the above |

Advantages: code reuse (write common code once), easier maintenance (fix it in the base class), extensibility (add a new subclass without touching tested code), and runtime polymorphism through base-class pointers or references.
Limitation: the derived class is tightly coupled to the base, so a change in the base can ripple into every subclass; deep hierarchies become hard to follow.

model_answer_bn:
সংজ্ঞা: Inheritance হলো OOP-এর সেই পদ্ধতি যার মাধ্যমে একটি নতুন class (derived class, child বা subclass) একটি বিদ্যমান class-এর (base class, parent বা superclass) data member ও member function পায় এবং নিজের member যোগ বা override করে। এটি "is-a" সম্পর্ক বোঝায়: একজন Officer একজন Employee।

Syntax: class Derived : access-mode Base { ... };   (C++)   class Derived extends Base { ... }   (Java)

| পরিভাষা | উদাহরণে অর্থ |
|---|---|
| Base class | Employee (name, basic, show()) |
| Derived class | Officer (houseRent ও gross() যোগ করে) |
| Inherited member | name, basic ও show() Officer নতুন করে না লিখেই ব্যবহার করে |
| Base constructor call | Officer(...) : Employee(n, b) আগে Employee অংশ তৈরি করে |

উদাহরণ (C++):

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Employee {                       // base class
protected:
    string name;
    double basic;
public:
    Employee(string n, double b) : name(n), basic(b) {}
    void show() const { cout << name << ", basic " << basic << endl; }
};

class Officer : public Employee {      // derived class
    double houseRent;
public:
    Officer(string n, double b) : Employee(n, b), houseRent(b * 0.5) {}
    double gross() const { return basic + houseRent; }   // uses inherited basic
};

int main() {
    Officer o("Nusrat", 40000);
    o.show();                          // inherited from Employee
    cout << "Gross: " << o.gross() << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Nusrat, basic 40000
Gross: 60000

Officer কখনো name, basic বা show() ঘোষণা করেনি; এগুলো Employee থেকে পুনরায় ব্যবহার করে এবং শুধু নতুন অংশ যোগ করে (basic-এর 50% house rent, তাই gross = 40000 + 20000 = 60000)। basic protected, তাই derived class এটি পড়তে পারে কিন্তু বাইরের code পারে না।

Inheritance-এর প্রকারভেদ

| প্রকার | গঠন |
|---|---|
| Single | A → B |
| Multilevel | A → B → C |
| Hierarchical | A → B, A → C |
| Multiple | A, B → C (শুধু C++; Java interface ব্যবহার করে) |
| Hybrid | উপরের দুই বা ততোধিক ধরনের মিশ্রণ |

সুবিধা: code reuse (সাধারণ code একবার লেখা), সহজ রক্ষণাবেক্ষণ (base class-এ ঠিক করলেই হয়), সম্প্রসারণযোগ্যতা (পরীক্ষিত code না ছুঁয়ে নতুন subclass যোগ), এবং base-class pointer বা reference দিয়ে runtime polymorphism।
সীমাবদ্ধতা: derived class base-এর সঙ্গে শক্তভাবে যুক্ত, তাই base-এ পরিবর্তন প্রতিটি subclass-এ ছড়িয়ে পড়তে পারে; গভীর hierarchy বোঝা কঠিন হয়।

model_answer_en:
Class: a user-defined type that bundles data members and member functions; it is a blueprint and takes no memory for data by itself (class Student { ... };).
Object: an instance of a class created from that blueprint; each object has its own copy of the data members (Student s1, s2;).

Constructor: a special member function with the same name as the class that runs automatically when an object is created, to initialise it.
Destructor: a special member function named ~ClassName that runs automatically when an object is destroyed, to release what it holds.

| Point | Constructor | Destructor |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Initialises the new object | Cleans up before the object's memory is released |
| Name | Same as the class: Room() | Tilde plus class name: ~Room() |
| When called | When the object is created (definition, new) | When it goes out of scope or delete is applied |
| Parameters | Can take parameters | Takes no parameters |
| Overloading | Can be overloaded (default, parameterised, copy) | Cannot be overloaded: exactly one per class |
| virtual | Cannot be virtual | Can be virtual (and should be in a polymorphic base) |
| Order in inheritance | Base first, then derived | Derived first, then base |
| Return type | None | None |

Example (C++):

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Room {
    int number;
public:
    Room(int n) {                 // constructor
        number = n;
        cout << "Room " << number << " booked" << endl;
    }
    ~Room() {                     // destructor
        cout << "Room " << number << " released" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Room r1(101);
    Room r2(102);
    cout << "Guests are staying" << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Room 101 booked
Room 102 booked
Guests are staying
Room 102 released
Room 101 released

r1 and r2 are constructed in order of definition and destroyed in reverse order when main ends.
Note: Java has constructors but no destructors; the garbage collector reclaims unreachable objects, and resources are closed with try-with-resources.

model_answer_bn:
Class: ব্যবহারকারী-সংজ্ঞায়িত একটি type, যা data member ও member function একসাথে রাখে; এটি একটি নকশা (blueprint) এবং নিজে data-র জন্য memory নেয় না (class Student { ... };)।
Object: সেই নকশা থেকে তৈরি class-এর একটি instance; প্রতিটি object-এর data member-এর নিজস্ব copy থাকে (Student s1, s2;)।

Constructor: class-এর নামের একটি বিশেষ member function, যা object তৈরির সময় নিজে থেকেই চলে এবং object-কে initialise করে।
Destructor: ~ClassName নামের একটি বিশেষ member function, যা object ধ্বংসের সময় নিজে থেকেই চলে এবং object-এর ধরে রাখা resource ছেড়ে দেয়।

| বিষয় | Constructor | Destructor |
|---|---|---|
| উদ্দেশ্য | নতুন object-কে initialise করে | Object-এর memory ছাড়ার আগে পরিষ্কার করে |
| নাম | Class-এর নামের মতো: Room() | Tilde ও class-এর নাম: ~Room() |
| কখন call হয় | Object তৈরির সময় (definition, new) | Scope-এর বাইরে গেলে বা delete করলে |
| Parameter | Parameter নিতে পারে | কোনো parameter নেয় না |
| Overloading | Overload করা যায় (default, parameterised, copy) | Overload করা যায় না: প্রতি class-এ ঠিক একটি |
| virtual | Virtual হতে পারে না | Virtual হতে পারে (polymorphic base-এ হওয়া উচিত) |
| Inheritance-এ ক্রম | আগে base, তারপর derived | আগে derived, তারপর base |
| Return type | নেই | নেই |

উদাহরণ (C++):

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Room {
    int number;
public:
    Room(int n) {                 // constructor
        number = n;
        cout << "Room " << number << " booked" << endl;
    }
    ~Room() {                     // destructor
        cout << "Room " << number << " released" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Room r1(101);
    Room r2(102);
    cout << "Guests are staying" << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Room 101 booked
Room 102 booked
Guests are staying
Room 102 released
Room 101 released

r1 ও r2 definition-এর ক্রমে তৈরি হয় এবং main শেষে উল্টো ক্রমে ধ্বংস হয়।
নোট: Java-তে constructor আছে কিন্তু destructor নেই; garbage collector পৌঁছানো যায় না এমন object ফেরত নেয়, আর resource বন্ধ করা হয় try-with-resources দিয়ে।

model_answer_en:
Definition: The diamond problem arises when a class inherits from two classes that both derive from the same base. The hierarchy forms a diamond:

        Device
        /    \
   Scanner  Printer
        \    /
        Copier

Without help, Copier contains two separate Device subobjects (one through Scanner, one through Printer). This causes:
- Duplicate data: two copies of every Device member, wasting memory and letting them hold different values.
- Ambiguity: c.serial does not compile ("non-static member 'serial' found in multiple base-class subobjects of type 'Device'"), and a Copier* cannot be converted to Device* without saying which path.

| Approach | How | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Scope resolution | c.Scanner::serial = 7; | Compiles, but there are still two Device copies |
| Virtual base class | class Scanner : virtual public Device, class Printer : virtual public Device | One shared Device subobject; c.serial is unambiguous |

Solution with virtual base classes:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Device {
public:
    int serial;
    Device() { serial = 0; cout << "Device "; }
};
class Scanner : virtual public Device {
public:
    Scanner() { cout << "Scanner "; }
};
class Printer : virtual public Device {
public:
    Printer() { cout << "Printer "; }
};
class Copier : public Scanner, public Printer {
public:
    Copier() { cout << "Copier" << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Copier c;
    c.serial = 7;              // unambiguous: only one Device part
    cout << c.serial << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Device Scanner Printer Copier
7

Device's constructor runs only once, before Scanner and Printer, because the most-derived class (Copier) constructs the shared virtual base.
Note: Java avoids the problem by not allowing a class to extend more than one class; it allows multiple interfaces, and a clash between two default methods must be resolved by overriding.

model_answer_bn:
সংজ্ঞা: Diamond problem দেখা দেয় যখন একটি class এমন দুটি class থেকে inherit করে, যারা দুজনেই একই base থেকে derived। Hierarchy-টি একটি diamond-এর আকার নেয়:

        Device
        /    \
   Scanner  Printer
        \    /
        Copier

কিছু না করলে Copier-এ দুটি আলাদা Device subobject থাকে (একটি Scanner হয়ে, একটি Printer হয়ে)। ফলে:
- দ্বিগুণ data: প্রতিটি Device member-এর দুটি copy, memory নষ্ট হয় এবং দুটিতে আলাদা মান থাকতে পারে।
- দ্ব্যর্থতা (ambiguity): c.serial compile হয় না ("non-static member 'serial' found in multiple base-class subobjects of type 'Device'"), আর কোন পথ তা না বললে Copier*-কে Device*-এ রূপান্তর করা যায় না।

| পদ্ধতি | কীভাবে | ফল |
|---|---|---|
| Scope resolution | c.Scanner::serial = 7; | Compile হয়, কিন্তু Device-এর দুটি copy থেকেই যায় |
| Virtual base class | class Scanner : virtual public Device, class Printer : virtual public Device | একটি shared Device subobject; c.serial দ্ব্যর্থহীন |

Virtual base class দিয়ে সমাধান:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Device {
public:
    int serial;
    Device() { serial = 0; cout << "Device "; }
};
class Scanner : virtual public Device {
public:
    Scanner() { cout << "Scanner "; }
};
class Printer : virtual public Device {
public:
    Printer() { cout << "Printer "; }
};
class Copier : public Scanner, public Printer {
public:
    Copier() { cout << "Copier" << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Copier c;
    c.serial = 7;              // unambiguous: only one Device part
    cout << c.serial << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
Device Scanner Printer Copier
7

Device-এর constructor মাত্র একবার, Scanner ও Printer-এর আগে চলে, কারণ shared virtual base তৈরি করে সবচেয়ে derived class (Copier)।
নোট: Java একটি class-কে একাধিক class extend করতে না দিয়ে সমস্যাটি এড়ায়; একাধিক interface চলে, আর দুটি default method সংঘর্ষ হলে override করে সমাধান করতে হয়।

model_answer_en:
Definition: Access specifiers are the keywords private, protected and public that control which code may use a class member. They enforce data hiding: the class decides what is its public interface and what stays internal. In a class, members are private by default; in a struct, public.

| Specifier | Same class | Derived class | Friend | Outside code (e.g. main) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| private | Yes | No | Yes | No |
| protected | Yes | Yes | Yes | No |
| public | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |

Purpose
- private: hides the internal data so it changes only through the class's own functions (for example a balance changed only by deposit()).
- protected: hides members from the outside world but shares them with derived classes.
- public: the interface that every user of the class calls.

Effect of the inheritance mode on base members

| Base member | public inheritance | protected inheritance | private inheritance |
|---|---|---|---|
| public | public | protected | private |
| protected | protected | protected | private |
| private | not accessible | not accessible | not accessible |

Example:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
private:   int a = 1;
protected: int b = 2;
public:    int c = 3;
    int getA() const { return a; }
};

class Child : public Base {
public:
    void show() const {
        // cout << a;   // error: a is private to Base
        cout << getA() << " " << b << " " << c << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Child ch;
    ch.show();
    cout << ch.c << endl;      // public: allowed
    // cout << ch.b;           // error: b is protected
    return 0;
}

Output:
1 2 3
3

Child reaches the private a only through the public getA(), uses the protected b directly, and main can use only the public c.

model_answer_bn:
সংজ্ঞা: Access specifier হলো private, protected ও public keyword, যা নির্ধারণ করে কোন code একটি class member ব্যবহার করতে পারবে। এগুলো data hiding নিশ্চিত করে: class নিজেই ঠিক করে কোনটি তার public interface আর কোনটি ভেতরের। Class-এ member ডিফল্টভাবে private; struct-এ public।

| Specifier | একই class | Derived class | Friend | বাইরের code (যেমন main) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| private | হ্যাঁ | না | হ্যাঁ | না |
| protected | হ্যাঁ | হ্যাঁ | হ্যাঁ | না |
| public | হ্যাঁ | হ্যাঁ | হ্যাঁ | হ্যাঁ |

উদ্দেশ্য
- private: ভেতরের data লুকিয়ে রাখে, যাতে শুধু class-এর নিজের function দিয়ে বদলায় (যেমন balance শুধু deposit() দিয়ে বদলায়)।
- protected: বাইরের জগৎ থেকে লুকায়, কিন্তু derived class-এর সঙ্গে ভাগ করে।
- public: class-এর প্রত্যেক ব্যবহারকারী যে interface call করে।

Base member-এর ওপর inheritance mode-এর প্রভাব

| Base member | public inheritance | protected inheritance | private inheritance |
|---|---|---|---|
| public | public | protected | private |
| protected | protected | protected | private |
| private | প্রবেশযোগ্য নয় | প্রবেশযোগ্য নয় | প্রবেশযোগ্য নয় |

উদাহরণ:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
private:   int a = 1;
protected: int b = 2;
public:    int c = 3;
    int getA() const { return a; }
};

class Child : public Base {
public:
    void show() const {
        // cout << a;   // error: a is private to Base
        cout << getA() << " " << b << " " << c << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Child ch;
    ch.show();
    cout << ch.c << endl;      // public: allowed
    // cout << ch.b;           // error: b is protected
    return 0;
}

Output:
1 2 3
3

Child private a-তে শুধু public getA() দিয়ে পৌঁছায়, protected b সরাসরি ব্যবহার করে, আর main শুধু public c ব্যবহার করতে পারে।

model_answer_en:
Definition: A copy constructor creates a new object as a copy of an existing object of the same class. Its form is ClassName(const ClassName &other). It runs for Name b = a;, Name b(a);, when an object is passed by value, and (unless elided) when one is returned by value.

| Point | Shallow copy | Deep copy |
|---|---|---|
| What is copied | Each member's value, including pointer addresses | The data a pointer points to, into newly allocated memory |
| Who provides it | The compiler-generated copy constructor | A copy constructor the programmer writes |
| After copying | Both objects point to the same buffer | Each object owns its own buffer |
| Changing one object | Also changes the other | Does not affect the other |
| Destruction | Both destructors delete the same memory (double delete, a crash) | Each destructor frees its own memory safely |

Example (deep copy):

#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
using namespace std;

class Name {
    char *text;
public:
    Name(const char *s) {
        text = new char[strlen(s) + 1];
        strcpy(text, s);
    }
    Name(const Name &other) {                 // deep copy
        text = new char[strlen(other.text) + 1];
        strcpy(text, other.text);
    }
    ~Name() { delete[] text; }
    void set(char c) { text[0] = c; }
    void print() const { cout << text << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Name a("Dhaka");
    Name b = a;          // copy constructor runs
    b.set('T');
    a.print();
    b.print();
    return 0;
}

Output:
Dhaka
Thaka

Because the copy constructor allocates a new buffer for b, changing b's first letter leaves a as Dhaka. If that copy constructor is removed, the compiler's shallow copy makes both pointers share "Dhaka": a would also print Thaka, and both destructors would delete[] the same block.

When to write one: whenever the class owns a resource through a pointer or handle (heap memory, a file, a socket). By the rule of three, such a class also needs its own destructor and copy assignment operator.
Limitation: deep copying costs time and memory for large objects; C++11 move constructors avoid the copy when the source is a temporary.

model_answer_bn:
সংজ্ঞা: Copy constructor একই class-এর একটি বিদ্যমান object-এর copy হিসেবে নতুন object তৈরি করে। এর রূপ ClassName(const ClassName &other)। এটি চলে Name b = a;, Name b(a);, object by value পাঠালে, এবং (elide না হলে) by value ফেরত দিলে।

| বিষয় | Shallow copy | Deep copy |
|---|---|---|
| কী copy হয় | প্রতিটি member-এর মান, pointer-এর address-সহ | Pointer যে data নির্দেশ করে, নতুন বরাদ্দ memory-তে |
| কে দেয় | Compiler-এর তৈরি copy constructor | Programmer-এর লেখা copy constructor |
| Copy-র পরে | দুটি object একই buffer নির্দেশ করে | প্রতিটি object-এর নিজস্ব buffer |
| একটি বদলালে | অন্যটিও বদলায় | অন্যটির ওপর প্রভাব নেই |
| Destruction | দুটি destructor একই memory delete করে (double delete, crash) | প্রতিটি destructor নিরাপদে নিজের memory ছাড়ে |

উদাহরণ (deep copy):

#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
using namespace std;

class Name {
    char *text;
public:
    Name(const char *s) {
        text = new char[strlen(s) + 1];
        strcpy(text, s);
    }
    Name(const Name &other) {                 // deep copy
        text = new char[strlen(other.text) + 1];
        strcpy(text, other.text);
    }
    ~Name() { delete[] text; }
    void set(char c) { text[0] = c; }
    void print() const { cout << text << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Name a("Dhaka");
    Name b = a;          // copy constructor runs
    b.set('T');
    a.print();
    b.print();
    return 0;
}

Output:
Dhaka
Thaka

Copy constructor b-এর জন্য নতুন buffer বরাদ্দ করে, তাই b-এর প্রথম অক্ষর বদলালেও a থাকে Dhaka। ওই copy constructor সরিয়ে দিলে compiler-এর shallow copy-তে দুটি pointer একই "Dhaka" ভাগ করত: a-ও Thaka দেখাত, এবং দুটি destructor একই block-এ delete[] করত।

কখন লিখতে হয়: যখনই class pointer বা handle দিয়ে কোনো resource (heap memory, file, socket) ধরে রাখে। Rule of three অনুযায়ী এমন class-এ নিজস্ব destructor ও copy assignment operator-ও লাগে।
সীমাবদ্ধতা: বড় object-এর deep copy-তে সময় ও memory খরচ হয়; source temporary হলে C++11-এর move constructor copy এড়ায়।

model_answer_en:
(a) Design: a SavingsAccount is an Account, so SavingsAccount derives publicly from Account.

| Class | Members | Access | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| Account | accNo, balance | protected | Hidden from outside code, but the subclass needs balance to compute profit |
| Account | constructor, monthlyProfit(), destructor | public | The interface the rest of the system uses |
| SavingsAccount | rate | private | Only the savings logic uses it |
| SavingsAccount | constructor, monthlyProfit() override | public | Interface |

(b) and (c) Program:

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Account {
protected:
    string accNo;
    double balance;
public:
    Account(string no, double b) : accNo(no), balance(b) {
        cout << "Account " << accNo << " opened" << endl;
    }
    virtual double monthlyProfit() const { return 0; }
    virtual ~Account() { cout << "Account " << accNo << " closed" << endl; }
};

class SavingsAccount : public Account {
    double rate;                       // yearly profit rate in percent
public:
    SavingsAccount(string no, double b, double r) : Account(no, b), rate(r) {
        cout << "Savings set up" << endl;
    }
    double monthlyProfit() const override { return balance * rate / 100 / 12; }
    ~SavingsAccount() { cout << "Savings record removed" << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Account *p = new SavingsAccount("SB-1001", 120000, 6);
    cout << "Profit: " << p->monthlyProfit() << endl;
    delete p;                           // runs both destructors
    return 0;
}

Output:
Account SB-1001 opened
Savings set up
Profit: 600
Savings record removed
Account SB-1001 closed

Constructors: SavingsAccount(...) : Account(no, b), rate(r) calls the base constructor in its initializer list. Account has no default constructor, so this call is required; the base part is built first ("Account SB-1001 opened"), then the derived part ("Savings set up").
Profit: 120000 x 6 / 100 / 12 = 600 Tk, chosen at run time through the Account* because monthlyProfit() is virtual.
Virtual destructor: the list stores Account*. If ~Account() were not virtual, delete p would be undefined behaviour and in practice would run only ~Account(), so the savings part ("Savings record removed") would never be cleaned up. With virtual ~Account(), delete p runs ~SavingsAccount() first and then ~Account(), the reverse of construction.

Advantage: new account types (current, fixed deposit) can be added as new subclasses without changing the code that processes the list. Limitation: every subclass depends on Account's protected layout, so changing it affects all of them.

