model_answer_en:
Array of objects: an array whose every element is an object of a class, as in Point pts[3];. Each element is constructed when the array is created and destroyed when the array goes out of scope.
Pointer to an object: a variable holding the address of an object, as in Point *p = &pts[0];. Members are reached with the arrow operator: p->move(10) means (*p).move(10).

Why a default constructor: Point pts[3]; gives no arguments for the elements, so the compiler must call Point() for each one. If the class declares only Point(int, int), the declaration fails to compile; either add Point() or initialise every element, and any element left without an initialiser still uses Point().

| Operation | Syntax | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| Stack array | Point pts[3]; | 3 constructor calls now, 3 destructor calls at end of scope |
| Pointer to element | Point *p = pts; | p points to pts[0]; p + 1 points to pts[1] |
| Member through pointer | p->move(10); | Same as (*p).move(10) |
| Heap array | Point *q = new Point[2]; | Allocates 2 objects and runs Point() twice |
| Release heap array | delete[] q; | Runs both destructors, then frees the memory |

Program:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
    int x, y;
public:
    Point() : x(0), y(0) {}                  // needed for Point pts[3]
    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}
    void move(int dx) { x += dx; }
    void show() const { cout << "(" << x << "," << y << ") "; }
};

int main() {
    Point pts[3] = { Point(1, 2), Point(3, 4) };  // pts[2] uses the default constructor
    Point *p = pts;                         // pointer to the first object
    p->move(10);                            // arrow operator
    (p + 1)->move(5);                       // next object in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) pts[i].show();
    cout << endl;

    Point *q = new Point[2];                // array of objects on the heap
    q[1] = Point(7, 8);
    q[1].show();
    delete[] q;                             // releases both objects
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:
(11,2) (8,4) (0,0) 
(7,8)

p->move(10) adds 10 to pts[0].x (1 → 11), and (p + 1)->move(5) adds 5 to pts[1].x (3 → 8). pts[2] had no initialiser, so the default constructor made it (0,0).
Note: memory from new[] must be freed with delete[], never plain delete or free(). In Java, Student[] s = new Student[3]; creates only 3 null references, and each object must then be made with new.

model_answer_bn:
Object-এর array: এমন একটি array যার প্রতিটি element একটি class-এর object, যেমন Point pts[3];। Array তৈরির সময় প্রতিটি element তৈরি হয় এবং array scope-এর বাইরে গেলে ধ্বংস হয়।
Object-এর pointer: একটি object-এর address ধারণকারী variable, যেমন Point *p = &pts[0];। Member-এ পৌঁছাতে arrow operator লাগে: p->move(10) মানে (*p).move(10)।

কেন default constructor: Point pts[3]; element-গুলোর জন্য কোনো argument দেয় না, তাই compiler-কে প্রতিটির জন্য Point() call করতে হয়। Class-এ শুধু Point(int, int) থাকলে ঘোষণাটি compile হয় না; হয় Point() যোগ করতে হবে, নয়তো প্রতিটি element initialise করতে হবে, আর initialiser ছাড়া থাকা element তবুও Point() ব্যবহার করে।

| কাজ | Syntax | প্রভাব |
|---|---|---|
| Stack array | Point pts[3]; | এখন 3টি constructor call, scope শেষে 3টি destructor call |
| Element-এর pointer | Point *p = pts; | p, pts[0]-কে নির্দেশ করে; p + 1, pts[1]-কে |
| Pointer দিয়ে member | p->move(10); | (*p).move(10)-এর সমান |
| Heap array | Point *q = new Point[2]; | 2টি object বরাদ্দ করে এবং Point() দুবার চালায় |
| Heap array মুক্ত | delete[] q; | দুটি destructor চালিয়ে memory ছেড়ে দেয় |

Program:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
