model_answer_en:
Definition: Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a paradigm that builds a program from objects, each holding its own data (state) and the methods that work on that data (behaviour); objects cooperate by sending messages, as in acc.deposit(500).

Building blocks
- Class: a blueprint or user-defined type that declares fields and methods.
- Object: an instance of a class, with identity, state and behaviour.

The four pillars (A PIE)

| Concept | Meaning | Java tool | Everyday picture |
|---|---|---|---|
| Abstraction | Show what an object does, hide how it does it | abstract class, interface, public methods | A car's steering and brake; the engine is hidden |
| Encapsulation | Bundle data with its methods in one unit and control access | class, private fields, getters/setters | A medicine capsule |
| Inheritance | A new class acquires and extends the members of an existing one (is-a) | extends (Java), : public (C++) | Savings is-a Account |
| Polymorphism | One interface, many forms: the same call behaves by type | overloading (compile time), overriding (run time) | monthlyProfit() differs for Savings and Current |

Example (all four in one program)

abstract class Account {                       // abstraction: what an account does
    private double balance;                    // encapsulation: data hidden
    protected Account(double opening) { balance = opening; }
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
    protected void add(double amt) { balance += amt; }
    public abstract double monthlyProfit();    // each kind decides how
}

class Savings extends Account {                // inheritance: Savings is-a Account
    Savings(double opening) { super(opening); }
    public double monthlyProfit() { return getBalance() * 0.06 / 12; }
}

class Current extends Account {
    Current(double opening) { super(opening); }
    public double monthlyProfit() { return 0; }
}

public class Main {
    static void deposit(Account a, double amt) { a.add(amt); }               // overloading:
    static void deposit(Account a, double amt, String note) { a.add(amt); }  // compile-time polymorphism

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account[] list = { new Savings(100000), new Current(50000) };
        deposit(list[0], 20000);
        deposit(list[1], 2000, "cash");
        for (Account a : list)                 // overriding: run-time polymorphism
            System.out.println(a.getClass().getSimpleName() + " " + a.getBalance()
                               + " profit " + a.monthlyProfit());
    }
}

Output:
Savings 120000.0 profit 600.0
Current 52000.0 profit 0.0

- Abstraction: Account declares monthlyProfit() without saying how.
- Encapsulation: balance is private and changes only through add().
- Inheritance: Savings and Current extend Account and reuse its code.
- Polymorphism: deposit() is overloaded, and monthlyProfit() is overridden and chosen at run time.

Benefit and limitation: together these give reuse, data security and easy extension (a new account type needs no change to old code). The cost is more design effort and some run-time indirection, so OOP pays off mainly for large, long-lived systems.

