model_answer_en:
Definitions
- Class: a user-defined type that acts as a blueprint; it declares data members (fields) and member functions (methods).
- Object: an instance of a class, created from the blueprint, with its own copy of the fields.

Declaring a class: access modifier, the keyword class, a name, and a body in braces holding fields, constructors and methods.

Creating objects in Java: ClassName ref = new ClassName(arguments); new allocates the object on the heap and runs the constructor. Declaring the reference alone creates no object.

public class Book {
    private String title;
    private double price;

    public Book(String title, double price) {   // constructor runs on new
        this.title = title;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public void show() { System.out.println(title + " - Tk " + price); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Book b1 = new Book("Padma Nadir Majhi", 250);   // declare + create
        Book b2;                                         // reference only, no object yet
        b2 = new Book("Lalsalu", 180);                   // object created here
        Book b3 = b1;                                    // second reference, same object
        b1.show();
        b2.show();
        System.out.println(b3 == b1);
    }
}

Output:
Padma Nadir Majhi - Tk 250.0
Lalsalu - Tk 180.0
true

Creating objects in C++: Book b1("...", 250); makes an object on the stack, and new Book(...) makes one on the heap that must be freed with delete.

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Book {
    string title;              // private by default
    double price;
public:
    Book(string t, double p) : title(t), price(p) {}
    void show() const { cout << title << " - Tk " << price << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Book b1("Padma Nadir Majhi", 250);        // object on the stack
    Book *b2 = new Book("Lalsalu", 180);      // object on the heap
    b1.show();
    b2->show();
    delete b2;                                 // the programmer must free heap objects
    return 0;
}

| Point | Class | Object |
|---|---|---|
| Nature | Blueprint, logical entity | Instance, physical entity |
| Memory | No field memory when declared | Memory allocated on creation |
| Declared / created | Once, with class | Many times, with new or a declaration (C++) |
| Contains | Definitions of fields and methods | Actual field values (state) |
| Example | Book | b1, b2 |

Note: b3 = b1 adds a second reference, not a second object, so b3 == b1 is true.

