model_answer_en:
Definition: An interface is a reference type that lists what a class must do, as method signatures, without saying how. A class that implements it promises to supply those methods. Its fields are constants (public static final), and since Java 8 it may also have default and static methods.

Uses of an interface
- A contract: every implementing class must provide the listed methods, so different teams can work against one agreed shape.
- Full abstraction: callers see only the operations, never the implementation.
- Multiple inheritance of type: a Java class extends one class but may implement many interfaces.
- Loose coupling: code depends on the interface, so an implementation can be swapped without changing the caller.
- Polymorphism: one interface reference can hold objects of many classes.
- Lambdas: an interface with one abstract method (a functional interface) can be implemented with a lambda.

Example (a bill-payment module that does not care how a bill is paid)

interface Payable {                       // the contract: what, not how
    double FEE_CAP = 25.0;                // implicitly public static final
    boolean pay(String bill, double amount);
    default String receipt(String bill) { return "Paid " + bill; }
}

class MobileWallet implements Payable {
    public boolean pay(String bill, double amount) {
        System.out.println("Wallet pays " + bill + " Tk " + amount);
        return true;
    }
}

class BankCard implements Payable {
    public boolean pay(String bill, double amount) {
