model_answer_en:
A **virtual function** is a base-class member function declared with the keyword `virtual`, so that a call through a base-class pointer or reference is resolved at RUN TIME to the overriding function in the derived class (dynamic dispatch). It is the mechanism behind run-time polymorphism, implemented with a vtable (per class) and a vptr (per object).

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Shape { public: virtual void area(){ cout << "Shape area\n"; } };
class Circle : public Shape { public: void area() override { cout << "Circle area\n"; } };
int main(){
    Shape* s = new Circle();
    s->area();          // dynamic dispatch -> "Circle area"
    delete s;
}
```
Output: `Circle area`. Without `virtual`, `s->area()` would call `Shape::area` (static binding). A polymorphic base class should also have a virtual destructor.

