model_answer_en:
**Exception handling** is the mechanism that detects and handles run-time errors so a program recovers gracefully instead of crashing.

- **try** — wraps the code that might raise an error.
- **throw** — signals (raises) an exception object.
- **catch** — handles an exception of a matching type.
- **finally** (Java) — a block that ALWAYS runs, used for cleanup.
- (**throws** declares the exceptions a method may propagate.)

```java
public class Demo {
  public static void main(String[] a){
    try {
      int r = 10 / 0;                       // throws ArithmeticException
      System.out.println(r);
    } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
      System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
    } finally {
      System.out.println("done");
    }
  }
}
```
Output:
```
Cannot divide by zero
done
```
Benefits: separates error-handling code from normal logic, lets errors propagate up the call stack, and guarantees cleanup via finally.

