model_answer_en:
A **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process — an independent sequence of instructions that the CPU scheduler can run. Threads of one process share its memory (code, data, heap) but each keeps its own stack and program counter.

**Two ways to create a thread in Java:**
1. **Extend `Thread`** and override `run()`, then call `start()`.
```java
class T extends Thread { public void run(){ System.out.println("run"); } }
new T().start();
```
2. **Implement `Runnable`** and pass it to a `Thread`.
```java
class R implements Runnable { public void run(){ System.out.println("run"); } }
new Thread(new R()).start();
```
Implementing Runnable is usually preferred because the class can still extend another class.

