```java
class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    synchronized void inc(){ count++; }      // serialised -> no lost update
    int get(){ return count; }
}
public class SyncCounter {
    public static void main(String[] a) throws InterruptedException {
        Counter c = new Counter();
        Runnable r = () -> { for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) c.inc(); };
        Thread t1 = new Thread(r), t2 = new Thread(r);
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();               // wait for both
        System.out.println(c.get());
    }
}
```
**Exact output (compiled & run):**
```
200000
```
`synchronized` makes each increment atomic so no update is lost, and `join()` guarantees both threads finish before `get()`. Without `synchronized` the output would be a non-deterministic value <= 200000 (a race condition).

